**Proposition 13.5 (one finite local implementation, with solver width
paid).** On a valid A acquisition there is an exact implementation of
the routes, gap decoder and predictor installation using
$O(N^4+QN^2)$ rational operations and $O(N^3)$ simultaneous rational
slots, besides the actual source and the nonnumerical stores in §14.
For C the corresponding nominal routes and inherited serial inverse use
$O(N^4)$ rational operations and $O(N^3)$ rational slots, plus the
actual digital extraction and integer/frame work. These are attained
arithmetic-operation upper bounds, not bit bounds, optima or a uniform
conditioning claim. A's total off-image recognizer has no supplied
historical $Q$ and is not assigned the valid-image $Q$ bound.

**Proof.** The earned tree gives $O_I$ with at most $b(I+1)\le N^2$
rows. Sparse multiplication constructs the powers; the explicit forcing
coefficient construction (5.2) gives $D_I$. AAII Proposition 90.4 gives
the conservative matrix/forcing bounds, with $O(N^3)$ slots.
For A, on each successive reverse tree compute and cache its rational
inverse by finite pivoted elimination. At size $M$ this uses $O(M^3)$
operations and $O(M^2)$ scratch. There are at most $n\le N$ such
trees, giving $O(N^4)$ preprocessing in total, with one inverse arena
live at a time. Each of the $Q$ actual reverse Ticks then uses one
matrix-vector multiplication of at most $O(N^2)$ operations. Initial
route calculations and the $I$ present-to-historical inverse Ticks
fit within $O(N^4)$. The alpha doubling and counter work are finite and
included at actual width. This proves the A count on its valid image.
The termination proof for arbitrary candidates is Theorem 4.3, without
assuming a true $Q$ for rejected inputs.

C needs one reverse field solve per recovered successful letter by
AAII Theorem 85.1, rather than an unknown-gap loop. Its existing
Proposition 90.4 gives $O(N^4)$ rational work with the stated slots.
For each actual report (10.2), exact integer floor/division, shifts,
denominator construction and conversion to a rational are finite
additional operations. Computing the source's digital floor in (8.1)
also pays the actual-width numerator/denominator arithmetic; it is not
a unit-cost exact-real measurement. $\square$

Let $W$ be the maximum actual width of a numerator, denominator, integer
operand or counter in the chosen finite implementation, including all
elimination and construction intermediates. Let $\mathcal A(W)$ bound
the implementation's exact rational/integer operation cost at that
width, including reduction. The rational-work contributions above are
bounded by $O((N^4+QN^2)\mathcal A(W))$ for A and
$O(N^4\mathcal A(W))$ for C, with numerical storage $O(N^3W)$ bits.
Add all stated integer/digitizer, copying, framing, allocation and source
costs. Each finite run has finite $W$; the source-width bound in
Proposition 13.4 is not a bound for every inverse/elimination
intermediate. A gap count may be retained in binary at its actual
$\ell(q_i)$ width, but an exported literal Tick word has its actual
$Q$ Tick letters. Compressing the local counter does not make those
physical historical Ticks or an explicitly requested literal export free.

